Scott asks for a combinator \(\mathrm{union} : \mathbb{P}(\mathbb{P} D) \to \mathbb{P} D\) such that, on nested finite generators, \(\bigcup_{i<n}\downarrow\bigl(\bigcup_{j<m_i}\downarrow X_{ij}\bigr)\) is related to \(\bigcup_{k<q}\downarrow Y_k\) if and only if \(\forall i<n\,\forall j<m_i\,\exists k<q.\, X_{ij}\subseteq Y_k\), then whether this combinator is computable when \(D\) is effectively given, what \(\mathrm{union}(\{\{x\},\{y,z\}\})\) is, and whether \(\mathbb{P}(\mathbb{P} D)\) and \(\mathbb{P} D\) are in general isomorphic. `unionMap` defines the relation independently of generators: a double-power neighbourhood \(A\) is related to a power neighbourhood \(B\) when every neighbourhood belonging to a member of \(A\) is contained in some member of \(B\), and proves approximability by three checks — the masters are related because every neighbourhood lies under the master, an intersection of targets is related because the two covering neighbourhoods meet in a neighbourhood that returns to both targets by downward closure, and a smaller source with a larger target stays related because the condition only quantifies over membership. `Basic` records the equivalence with the two neighbourhood-membership side conditions, as a definitional identity. `unionMap_rel_fin` proves Scott's displayed biconditional: a generator of an inner union belongs to a member of the source union, so the relation supplies a covering neighbourhood lying under a generator of the target, and conversely a member of the source lies under an inner union and a neighbourhood inside it lies under a generator, so a covering generator's inclusion composes with that containment. `unionMap_rel_Ypd_iff` is the same biconditional on canonical codes, and `unionMap_isComputable` answers the computability question by a decidable predicate: the inner consistency decider is the constant function with value one, correct because the empty union coded by zero is contained in every neighbourhood, and the nested bounded quantification of code inclusion is decidable, hence recursively enumerable. `example_720` is the conjunction of the computability theorem, the code characterisation, and the finite-generator characterisation. The value on \(\{\{x\},\{y,z\}\}\) and the isomorphism question are not stated. **Theorem:** partial — the combinator, the finite-generator equivalence, and computability for an effectively given base are proved; the element computation and the isomorphism question are absent. **Proof:** partial — the relation and the decidability argument are proved; there is no calculation of the image of \(\{\{x\},\{y,z\}\}\) and no argument about whether \(\mathrm{union}\) is injective.
